<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  One grown citrus, 3.95 m across and 2.83 m to the top, skirted to the
  ground. Measured on the OBJ, not on the radii it was pushed out of: the
  lobes stand proud of that sphere by a fifth.

  A mesh rather than the ellipsoid, and the difference is what stands up to
  being looked at, from the alley or from a camera two metres away. It is what
  most of every farm is planted with, eight crowns to a baked run.

  One of four trees, and the four are the point: model://citrus_tree_fruit is
  this crown carrying oranges, model://citrus_round is the same envelope as a
  bare ellipsoid, and model://forest_tree is what the mata is made of. A
  talhao is planted with one of the first three and a mata with the fourth, so
  a world holds all four and any two of them can be compared inside it.

  Why the mesh looks like a tree and the ellipsoid does not is lighting rather
  than shape. The crown here is a sphere pushed out into eight foliage clumps
  and given a few centimetres of jitter on top of that, and the OBJ carries one
  normal per face instead of averaged vertex normals, so every facet takes the
  sun on its own: the pale clumps and the near black pockets between them come
  out of the light in the world, not out of the material. An ellipsoid is a
  smooth surface and can only ever take one smooth gradient.

  It costs one visual, the same as the ellipsoid does. Gazebo charges per
  entity and not per triangle, which is the measurement the farm worlds are
  built on, so 1296 triangles of canopy are free where a second shape would
  not be. Fruit is that second shape, and it lives in its own model for
  exactly that reason.

  The trunk is inside the canopy mesh. A skirted tree shows about 20 cm of it,
  which is not worth an entity of its own; it is brown rather than green
  because the colours come from the OBJ's own .mtl, and a visual that names a
  <material> would override both shapes with one of them.

  Six crowns live in meshes/, each with its own leaf green. A row planted from
  the six shuffled, turned to random yaw and scaled a few per cent either way
  is what stops a block from reading as one tree repeated.

  Nothing here collides, and none of the four tree models does. A grove
  simulated for perception does not need the solver to know where the trunks
  are: a camera and a GPU lidar both work off visuals, and the physics engine
  was being charged for cylinders nothing in a perception run ever touches.
  Driving through a tree is the deliberate trade — the ground, the shed and
  the machinery still collide, so a machine can still fall off nothing and
  still cannot drive through the yard.
-->
<sdf version="1.10">
  <model name="citrus_tree">
    <static>true</static>

    <link name="tree">
      <visual name="canopy">
        <geometry>
          <mesh><uri>model://citrus_tree/meshes/citrus_a.obj</uri></mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>

    </link>

  </model>
</sdf>
